DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The DTrace lloocckkssttaatt provider allows the tracing of events related to
     locking on FreeBSD.

     The ddttrraaccee__lloocckkssttaatt provider contains DTrace probes for inspecting kernel
     lock state transitions.  Probes exist for the lockmgr(9), mutex(9),
     rwlock(9), and sx(9) lock types.  The lockstat(1) utility can be used to
     collect and display data collected from the ddttrraaccee__lloocckkssttaatt provider.
     Each type of lock has aaccqquuiirree() and rreelleeaassee() probes which expose the
     lock structure being operated upon, as well as probes which fire when a
     thread contends with other threads for ownership of a lock.

     The lloocckkssttaatt::::::aaddaappttiivvee--aaccqquuiirree() and lloocckkssttaatt::::::aaddaappttiivvee--rreelleeaassee()
     probes fire when an MTX_DEF mutex(9) is acquired and released, respec‐
     tively.  The only argument is a pointer to the lock structure which de‐
     scribes the lock being acquired or released.

     The lloocckkssttaatt::::::aaddaappttiivvee--ssppiinn() probe fires when a thread spins while
     waiting for a MTX_DEF mutex(9) to be released by another thread.  The
     first argument is a pointer to the lock structure that describes the lock
     and the second argument is the amount of time, in nanoseconds, that the
     mutex spent spinning.  The lloocckkssttaatt::::::aaddaappttiivvee--bblloocckk() probe fires when a
     thread takes itself off the CPU while trying to acquire an MTX_DEF
     mutex(9) that is owned by another thread.  The first argument is a
     pointer to the lock structure that describes the lock and the second ar‐
     gument is the length of time, in nanoseconds, that the waiting thread was
     blocked.  The lloocckkssttaatt::::::aaddaappttiivvee--bblloocckk() and lloocckkssttaatt::::::aaddaappttiivvee--ssppiinn()
     probes fire only after the lock has been successfully acquired, and in
     particular, after the lloocckkssttaatt::::::aaddaappttiivvee--aaccqquuiirree() probe fires.

     The lloocckkssttaatt::::::ssppiinn--aaccqquuiirree() and lloocckkssttaatt::::::ssppiinn--rreelleeaassee() probes fire
     when a MTX_SPIN mutex(9) is acquired or released, respectively.  The only
     argument is a pointer to the lock structure which describes the lock be‐
     ing acquired or released.

     The lloocckkssttaatt::::::ssppiinn--ssppiinn() probe fires when a thread spins while waiting
     for a MTX_SPIN mutex(9) to be released by another thread.  The first ar‐
     gument is a pointer to the lock structure that describes the lock and the
     second argument is the length of the time spent spinning, in nanoseconds.
     The lloocckkssttaatt::::::ssppiinn--ssppiinn() probe fires only after the lock has been suc‐
     cessfully acquired, and in particular, after the lloocckkssttaatt::::::ssppiinn--
     aaccqquuiirree() probe fires.

     The lloocckkssttaatt::::::rrww--aaccqquuiirree() and lloocckkssttaatt::::::rrww--rreelleeaassee() probes fire when
     a rwlock(9) is acquired or released, respectively.  The first argument is
     a pointer to the structure which describes the lock being acquired.  The
     second argument is 0 if the lock is being acquired or released as a
     writer, and 1 if it is being acquired or released as a reader.  The
     lloocckkssttaatt::::::ssxx--aaccqquuiirree() and lloocckkssttaatt::::::ssxx--rreelleeaassee(), and
     lloocckkssttaatt::::::lloocckkmmggrr--aaccqquuiirree() and lloocckkssttaatt::::::lloocckkmmggrr--rreelleeaassee() probes fire
     upon the corresponding events for sx(9) and lockmgr(9) locks, respec‐
     tively.  The lloocckkssttaatt::::::lloocckkmmggrr--ddiissoowwnn() probe fires when a lockmgr(9)
     exclusive lock is disowned.  In this state, the lock remains exclusively
     held, but may be released by a different thread.  The lloocckkssttaatt::::::lloocckkmmggrr--
     rreelleeaassee() probe does not fire when releasing a disowned lock.  The first
     argument is a pointer to the structure which describes the lock being
     disowned.  The second argument is 0, for compatibility with
     lloocckkssttaatt::::::lloocckkmmggrr--rreelleeaassee().

     The lloocckkssttaatt::::::rrww--bblloocckk(), lloocckkssttaatt::::::ssxx--bblloocckk(), and lloocckkssttaatt::::::lloocckkmmggrr--
     bblloocckk() probes fire when a thread removes itself from the CPU while wait‐
     ing to acquire a lock of the corresponding type.  The lloocckkssttaatt::::::rrww--
     ssppiinn() and lloocckkssttaatt::::::ssxx--ssppiinn() probes fire when a thread spins while
     waiting to acquire a lock of the corresponding type.  All probes take the
     same set of arguments.  The first argument is a pointer to the lock
     structure that describes the lock.  The second argument is the length of
     time, in nanoseconds, that the waiting thread was off the CPU or spinning
     for the lock.  The third argument is 0 if the thread is attempting to ac‐
     quire the lock as a writer, and 1 if the thread is attempting to acquire
     the lock as a reader.  The fourth argument is 0 if the thread is waiting
     for a reader to release the lock, and 1 if the thread is waiting for a
     writer to release the lock.  The fifth argument is the number of readers
     that held the lock when the thread first attempted to acquire the lock.
     This argument will be 0 if the fourth argument is 1.

     The lloocckkssttaatt::::::lloocckkmmggrr--uuppggrraaddee(), lloocckkssttaatt::::::rrww--uuppggrraaddee(), and
     lloocckkssttaatt::::::ssxx--uuppggrraaddee() probes fire when a thread successfully upgrades a
     held lockmgr(9), rwlock(9), or sx(9) shared/reader lock to an exclu‐
     sive/writer lock.  The only argument is a pointer to the structure which
     describes the lock being acquired.  The lloocckkssttaatt::::::lloocckkmmggrr--ddoowwnnggrraaddee(),
     lloocckkssttaatt::::::rrww--ddoowwnnggrraaddee(), and lloocckkssttaatt::::::ssxx--ddoowwnnggrraaddee() probes fire when
     a thread downgrades a held lockmgr(9), rwlock(9), or sx(9) exclu‐
     sive/writer lock to a shared/reader lock.

     The lloocckkssttaatt::::::tthhrreeaadd--ssppiinn() probe fires when a thread spins on a thread
     lock, which is a specialized MTX_SPIN mutex(9).  The first argument is a
     pointer to the structure that describes the lock and the second argument
     is the length of time, in nanoseconds, that the thread was spinning.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     dtrace(1), lockstat(1), locking(9), mutex(9), rwlock(9), SDT(9), sx(9)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ddttrraaccee__lloocckkssttaatt provider first appeared in Solaris.  The FreeBSD im‐
     plementation of the ddttrraaccee__lloocckkssttaatt provider first appeared in FreeBSD 9.

